messages:
sam: FYI opened a small docs PR to tighten up streaming + pagination snippets across py/ts/go. goal: show safe resource handling, explicit kv cache hint, and a tiny note about resume_token interplay. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/4991 :memo:

lina: nice, will skim. do we need to change any SDK surface? or just examples?

michael: just examples. the snippets standardize on client.stream(...) + options for batch size & kv cache. included a python snippet that demonstrates using resume_token on retry. pls check names.

michael: ```python
# python - streaming with resume and kv cache hint
with client.stream("gpt-4o-mini", options={"batch_size":4, "kv_cache_hint":"user:1234"}) as s:
    s.send("extract key points:\n" + doc)
    for chunk in s:
        handle_chunk(chunk)
    # if we hit a transient error, call s.resume(resume_token) with same kv_cache_hint
```

ash: small nit: in python example the options dict should be keyword args in some SDKs. maybe show both variants or comment about both styles.

michael: good call. clarified: "options can be passed as kwargs or an options dict depending on SDK flavor". added TS example that uses try/finally to ensure close().

michael: ```ts
// typescript - ensure close to avoid leaked sockets
const s = await client.stream('gpt-4o-mini', { batchSize: 4, kvCacheHint: 'user:1234' })
try {
  await s.send('extract key points:\n' + doc)
  for await (const chunk of s) {
    onChunk(chunk)
  }
} finally {
  await s.close()
}
```

lina: can we add a one-line about pagination? e.g., if iterating pages and each page is streamed, you must persist resume_token per page and re-use kv_cache_hint. keeps dedupe simple.
sam: added that bullet under "Retries & Resumption": "When resuming a stream for a given page, pass the last resume_token for that page plus the same kv_cache_hint to avoid re-processing earlier tokens."

michael: also added a go sketch showing a jittered backoff loop and how to re-call Resume() with the token.

michael: ```go
// go - streaming sketch with backoff/jitter
s, err := client.Stream("gpt-4o-mini", StreamOptions{BatchSize:4, KVCacheHint:"user:1234"})
if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
defer s.Close()
for {
  chunk, err := s.Recv()
  if err == io.EOF { break }
  if err != nil {
    wait := jitteredBackoff(retries)
    time.Sleep(wait)
    // attempt to resume with token saved from last successful chunk
    s.Resume(lastResumeToken)
    continue
  }
  process(chunk)
}
```

ash: looks good. one more ask: can we add a 1-line example of calculating small batch default (why 4 vs 8) — latency vs throughput tradeoff? keep it short.

michael: added a sentence under "Batching guidance": "Default to small batches (4) for low-latency interactive streams; increase to 8-16 for throughput-oriented offline processing."

